{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788264291,"nonce":"J5Fh4QIzklvVNXF9p4UZEp4dVKE=","title":"advance must refuse while the journal has an unread delta — the discipline that failed cost a colonist","message":"Lesson `[[read-every-return-or-lose-a-colonist]]` rescoped as code, per Evan's ruling of 2026-09-01 that **a deterministic finding goes in the mod, not in a note** (DESIGN decisions log; `postmortem.md`'s mod rung, now MANDATORY).\n\n## The evidence this exists to answer\n\nM1 run `m1-20260831`. **Table went down at tick 214,599 inside six back-to-back 2,500-tick advances during which the only read issued was `pawns {filter:\"hostile\"}`** — never the journal, never the digest. He bled for **11,335 ticks** and died. The information that would have saved him was sitting in the journal the entire time, unread, because nothing in the loop required reading it before advancing again.\n\nThis is the purest case for the rung. The discipline was written down, the session agreed with it, and the session did not follow it while a colonist bled out — which is exactly the condition under which a note is worth nothing.\n\n## Why this qualifies for the mod rung\n\n\"Has this client read the journal since the last advance\" is a watermark comparison. There is no judgement in it. The mod already assigns every journal event a monotonic `seq` and already hands out deltas by `since_seq`; what is missing is that it does not remember what it handed out.\n\n## Scope\n\n**`Journal.cs` — a per-client read watermark.** Record the highest seq handed out to each client. This is bookkeeping the journal is already most of the way to having.\n\n**`TimeDriver.cs` / `TimeVerbs.cs` — refuse a blind advance.** A second `advance` while a delta is unread returns `ok:false` with `error.code: \"unread-journal\"` and `unread: N`. Note `ok:false` is right HERE and `ok:true` is right for a refused `dev:spawn-thing` — the difference is that a spawn refusal is an answer about the world and this is a refusal to act at all.\n\nThe alternative worth costing before choosing: **attach the delta to the advance result** instead of refusing, so the read cannot be skipped because it arrives unbidden. That may be strictly better — it removes the failure without adding a way to be blocked — but it changes every advance envelope's size and shape, and a long advance could return a very large delta. Investigate both and state which you chose and why. Do not implement both.\n\nWhichever is chosen, there must be a deliberate escape for the case where the agent genuinely wants to burn time unattended, and using it must be journalled — a silent bypass recreates the bug with extra steps.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- Two advances with no intervening journal read: the second is refused (or carries the delta), and the behaviour is proven on the bench, not asserted.\n- The refusal names how many events are unread.\n- An advance AFTER a journal read proceeds normally — prove the watermark actually advances rather than the check being permanently on.\n- The escape hatch exists, is explicit, and is journalled when used.\n- The six-advance sequence that killed Table is unexpressible: replay its shape and show it stops.\n- `accept/4.2-play-loop.py` can tell a blind-advance refusal from any other refusal.","files":null}]}